{{also|synæsthesia}}
==English==
{{was wotd|2019|May|1}}

===Etymology===
From {{der|en|grc|σύν||with}} + {{m|grc|αἴσθησις||sensation}}, modelled after {{m|en|anaesthesia}}. It is analysable as {{prefix|en|syn|aesthesia}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|RP}} {{IPA|/ˌsɪn.ɪsˈθiː.ʒə/|/ˌsɪn.ɪsˈθiː.zɪ.ə/|lang=en}}
* {{a|GA}} {{IPA|/ˌsɪn.əsˈθi.ʒə/|lang=en}}
* {{rhymes|iːʒə|lang=en}}
* {{hyphenation|syn|aes|thes|ia|lang=en}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# {{lb|en|neurology|psychology}} A [[neurological]] or [[psychological]] [[phenomenon]] whereby a particular [[sensory]] [[stimulus]] [[trigger#Verb|triggers]] a second kind of [[sensation]]. {{defdate|from late 19th c.}}
#* {{quote-book|author=William Gibson|authorlink=William Gibson|chapter=18|title={{w|Neuromancer}}|series=[[w:Sprawl trilogy|Sprawl]]|seriesvolume=book 1|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher={{w|Ace Books}}|date=1 July 1984|page=221|pageurl=https://archive.org/stream/neuromancer00gibs#page/221/mode/1up|isbn=978-0-441-56959-5|passage=Into her darkness, a churning '''synaesthesia''', where her pain was the taste of old iron, scent of melon, wings of a moth brushing her cheek.}}
#* {{quote-book|author=Sean A. Day|chapter=What Synaesthesia Is (and Is Not)|editors=Paul Mc Kevitt, Seán Ó Nualláin, and Conn Mulvihill|title=Language, Vision and Music: Selected Papers from the 8th International Workshop on the Cognitive Science of Natural Language Processing, Galway, Ireland 1999|series=Advances in Consciousness Research|seriesvolume=35|location=Amsterdam; Philadelphia, Pa.|publisher={{w|John Benjamins Publishing Company}}|year=2002|page=171|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=OyRZAQL0x6QC&pg=PA171|isbn=978-90-272-5155-8|issn=1381-589X|passage=For example, I myself have a type of '''synaesthesia''': The sounds of musical instruments will sometimes make me see colors, about a yard in front of me, each color specific and consistent with the particular instrument playing.}}
#* {{quote-book|author=Graham Richards|chapter=|title=Psychology: The Key Concepts|series=Key Concepts|location=London; New York, N.Y.|publisher={{w|Routledge}}|year=2009|page=244|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=_p_DYj_UdKcC&pg=PA244|isbn=978-0-415-43200-9|passage='''Synaesthesia''' can occur particularly powerfully during mescalin and LSD intoxication, and is often given mystical significance.}}
# {{lb|en|by extension}} The [[association]] of one sensory [[perception]] with, or [[description]] of it in terms of, a different perception that is not [[experience#Verb|experienced]] at the same time.
#* {{quote-book|author=Claude Lévi–Strauss|authorlink=Claude Lévi–Strauss|author2=Claire Jacobson and Brooke Grundfest Schoepf, transl.|chapter=Postscript to Chapters III and IV|title=Structural Anthropology: Translated from the French|series=Harper Torchbooks|seriesvolume=TB5017|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher={{w|Basic Books}}|year=1963|pages=91–92|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=2xaB9PksH1UC&pg=PA91|oclc=490234036|passage=On a phonemic level, phenomena of '''synesthesia''' have often been described and studied. Practically all children and a good many adults—though for the most part adults will deny it—spontaneously associate sounds, whether phonemes or the timbre of musical instruments, with colors and forms.}}
#* {{quote-book|author=Boris Wiseman|chapter=Structuralism, Symbolist Poetics and Abstract Art|title=Lévi-Strauss, Anthropology, and Aesthetics|series=Ideas in Context|seriesvolume=85|location=Cambridge|publisher={{w|Cambridge University Press}}|year=2007|page=112|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=pOJTWmu9rlMC&pg=PA112|isbn=978-0-521-87529-5|passage=For one of the enigmatic features of '''synaesthesia''' is that, within a given cultural group, the kinds of associations made by different subjects occur according to statistically verifiable recurring patterns. As Jakobson explains, ‘when we ask whether /i/ or /u/ is darker, testing such phonic oppositions as grave vs. acute, some of the subjects may respond that this question makes no sense to them, but hardly one will respond that /i/ is the darker of the two’{{nb...}}.}}
# {{lb|en|arts|literature}} A [[literary]] or [[artistic]] [[device]] whereby one kind of sensation is described in the terms of another.
#* {{quote-book|author=Stephen Bowkett|authorlink=Stephen Bowkett|chapter=Descriptive Writing|title=Boys and Writing|series=Pocket PAL|location=London|publisher=Network Continuum Education, {{w|Continuum International Publishing Group}}|year=2006|page=38|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=DGxa0WXX2B4C&pg=PT39|isbn=978-1-85539-108-6|passage=Linking moods with colours is one example of '''synaesthesia'''. {{...}} As a tool for improving writing, the idea of '''synaesthesia''' is versatile and innovative. Why use only visual references to describe visual impressions? Why just describe sounds using sound-related words? Play with cross-matching the senses.}}
#* {{quote-book|author=Antti-Ville Kärjä|chapter=Visions of a Sound Nation: Finnish Music Videos and Secured Otherness|editors=Roger Beebe and Jason Middleton|title=Medium Cool: Music Videos from Soundies to Cellphones|location=Durham, N.C.; London|publisher={{w|Duke University Press}}|year=2007|page=181|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=8f_-1giPRFoC&pg=PA181|isbn=978-0-8223-4139-0|passage=[I]t may be stated that the concept of '''synaesthesia''' is instrumental for understanding music videos, since videos are based on the soundtrack's visual associations. Yet to discuss associations here may cause some problems. {{...}} &#91;{{w|Nicholas Cook}}&#93; seems to be relying on a rather normative conception of '''synaesthesia''', which for its part supports the idea of absolute, nonrepresentational music. The implication of his discussion is that the "true" forms of music–image '''synaesthesia''' come only in the forms of perception of color or light when hearing a certain musical sound.}}

